[Music]  our series of more perfect union aims to  show that what unites us as americans is  far greater than what divides us  this morning we introduce you to a  world-class chess player bringing his  knowledge of the game  to under-served communities around st  louis  when he was 12 years old justice  williams was the youngest ever  african-american national chess master  now more than a decade later nancy chen  shows us  how he's how he's inspiring the next  generation  to think like a grand master is really  not that hard which pawn gets to the  other side of the board first  remember our goal is to think 10 moves  ahead when the lesson of the day is to  think  10 moves ahead each decision whether by  pawn or by queen has consequences  in this st louis neighborhood  23-year-old justice williams  spends his saturday mornings teaching  the centuries-old game  to a new generation of chess players  what's your favorite part about playing  chess i like to win a lot  and i hear you win quite a bit you beat  your dad  and a lot of other pronouns their  teacher  ranked among the top 1 percent of  players in the country  in 2010 as a 12-year-old williams became  the youngest  african-american national chess master  if you have a bishop it always stays on  its covers  partnering with a local non-profit  williams started the program last fall  to make chess more accessible for black  youth in the st louis area  its name black squares what is the  meaning behind black squares  to play a legal game of chess white  moves first and often that means that  players think that white has the  advantage but i find my own advantages  with black  advantages williams honed after joining  the chess club at his new york city  elementary school  you had a tough start in chess oh i had  an awful start like i used to go to  tournaments i would lose all my games  so you had never played chess before  that no did anyone in your family play  chess  no they didn't play together and nobody  definitely played with me  but william's real expertise came from  some unlikely  teachers i traveled from the bronx to  washington square park  um on the train by subway by myself i  would play those guys there  but i think that that time playing blitz  in the park really set me apart because  i was  dealing with adults and adult trash talk  the practice paid off williams spent his  teens traveling the world for  competitions  seventh place was justice williams  he was even featured in a documentary on  a junior high chess team  the game earning him a full college  scholarship in st  louis in life there's a lot of judgment  calls  and in chess you got to make a lot of  decisions so we're learning here just  how to analyze and interpret situations  and that's how you not only become not  the problem but also the solution  there's a lot of options out here it's  just what you choose to occupy your idle  time  that mission has led williams to open a  new world of queens and kings  to underserved communities of all kinds  of all this y'all gonna have to play  this man right here more than a hundred  inmates at the st louis county justice  center compete in chess tournaments  organized by williams i played some of  my cousins  who were previously incarcerated we  developed like a different kind of bond  from playing chess  and then talking to some of the players  in the park some of them are  incarcerated  and that's where they learn how to play  the game he picked up on a whim  as an eight-year-old becoming his life's  strategy  when i play chess i'm looking for what  my opponent is not looking for  the communities that we're targeting  we're targeting them because  nobody else is targeting them right  we're looking for the  solution that nobody else is looking for  a sign that despite his lesson for the  day  williams is thinking well more than 10  moves ahead  for cbs this morning nancy chen saint  louis  boy i like what he said life is a series  of judgment calls right justice  bravo yes makes me want to learn how to  play

